@anon.3c5d4856225436a016f648d8ca8332b3.2679 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [2591 x i8] }> <{ [2591 x i8] c"# `intrinsics`\0A\0AThe tracking issue for this feature is: None.\0A\0AIntrinsics are rarely intended to be stable directly, but are usually\0Aexported in some sort of stable manner. Prefer using the stable interfaces to\0Athe intrinsic directly when you can.\0A\0A------------------------\0A\0A\0A## Intrinsics with fallback logic\0A\0AMany intrinsics can be written in pure rust, albeit inefficiently or without supporting\0Asome features that only exist on some backends. Backends can simply not implement those\0Aintrinsics without causing any code miscompilations or failures to compile.\0AAll intrinsic fallback bodies are automatically made cross-crate inlineable (like `#[inline]`)\0Aby the codegen backend, but not the MIR inliner.\0A\0A```rust\0A#![feature(rustc_attrs, effects)]\0A#![allow(internal_features)]\0A\0A#[rustc_intrinsic]\0Aconst unsafe fn const_deallocate(_ptr: *mut u8, _size: usize, _align: usize) {}\0A```\0A\0ASince these are just regular functions, it is perfectly ok to create the intrinsic twice:\0A\0A```rust\0A#![feature(rustc_attrs, effects)]\0A#![allow(internal_features)]\0A\0A#[rustc_intrinsic]\0Aconst unsafe fn const_deallocate(_ptr: *mut u8, _size: usize, _align: usize) {}\0A\0Amod foo {\0A    #[rustc_intrinsic]\0A    const unsafe fn const_deallocate(_ptr: *mut u8, _size: usize, _align: usize) {\0A        panic!(\22noisy const dealloc\22)\0A    }\0A}\0A\0A```\0A\0AThe behaviour on backends that override the intrinsic is exactly the same. On other\0Abackends, the intrinsic behaviour depends on which implementation is called, just like\0Awith any regular function.\0A\0A## Intrinsics lowered to MIR instructions\0A\0AVarious intrinsics have native MIR operations that they correspond to. Instead of requiring\0Abackends to implement both the intrinsic and the MIR operation, the `lower_intrinsics` pass\0Awill convert the calls to the MIR operation. Backends do not need to know about these intrinsics\0Aat all.\0A\0A## Intrinsics without fallback logic\0A\0AThese must be implemented by all backends.\0A\0AThese are imported as if they were FFI functions, with the special\0A`rust-intrinsic` ABI. For example, if one was in a freestanding\0Acontext, but wished to be able to `transmute` between types, and\0Aperform efficient pointer arithmetic, one would import those functions\0Avia a declaration like\0A\0A```rust\0A#![feature(intrinsics)]\0A#![allow(internal_features)]\0A# fn main() {}\0A\0Aextern \22rust-intrinsic\22 {\0A    fn transmute<T, U>(x: T) -> U;\0A\0A    fn arith_offset<T>(dst: *const T, offset: isize) -> *const T;\0A}\0A```\0A\0AAs with any other FFI functions, these are by default always `unsafe` to call.\0AYou can add `#[rustc_safe_intrinsic]` to the intrinsic to make it safe to call.\0A" }>, align 1
