@anon.3c5d4856225436a016f648d8ca8332b3.2784 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [1221 x i8] }> <{ [1221 x i8] c"# `marker_trait_attr`\0A\0AThe tracking issue for this feature is: [#29864]\0A\0A[#29864]: https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/29864\0A\0A------------------------\0A\0ANormally, Rust keeps you from adding trait implementations that could\0Aoverlap with each other, as it would be ambiguous which to use.  This\0Afeature, however, carves out an exception to that rule: a trait can\0Aopt-in to having overlapping implementations, at the cost that those\0Aimplementations are not allowed to override anything (and thus the\0Atrait itself cannot have any associated items, as they're pointless\0Awhen they'd need to do the same thing for every type anyway).\0A\0A```rust\0A#![feature(marker_trait_attr)]\0A\0A#[marker] trait CheapToClone: Clone {}\0A\0Aimpl<T: Copy> CheapToClone for T {}\0A\0A// These could potentially overlap with the blanket implementation above,\0A// so are only allowed because CheapToClone is a marker trait.\0Aimpl<T: CheapToClone, U: CheapToClone> CheapToClone for (T, U) {}\0Aimpl<T: CheapToClone> CheapToClone for std::ops::Range<T> {}\0A\0Afn cheap_clone<T: CheapToClone>(t: T) -> T {\0A    t.clone()\0A}\0A```\0A\0AThis is expected to replace the unstable `overlapping_marker_traits`\0Afeature, which applied to all empty traits (without needing an opt-in).\0A" }>, align 1
